<<~ kanawai "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/ref/RFC-2119">>

! Founding Runbook ~ one household walk

<<~ ahu #/the-rite>>

!! 1 · What founding makes

A node founded by one operator stands as a SEED. A Nexus stands when two operators each seat their own key in a fresh charter (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/seed-and-nexus">>). One act founds the household's node, the Stable founding act (`lares vessel found --hearth`): under one human route it mints the custody, the place and persona zero, and a PRIVATE seed charter with one seat at threshold one, whose next key rests on a cold-key card; persona zero's face binds that charter's Nexus AID. No keyring rides it. The node then seeds its corpus from the tracked bags. No prior CRDT document needs to survive. Each operator founds their own node on their own machine. A browser counts as a leaf of its human's Lararium; each device mints its own key, one face per device, and no device receives a seed.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/fresh-state>>

!! Fresh state

A fresh founding needs only a fresh state mount and this household's tracked repository checkout at `/workspace`. For an older installation, move its homes aside by hand before founding again, as the stable founding road directs. No CLI pave or wipe command stands.

''Do'': prepare the first operator's fresh per-vessel `/state` directory and point `/workspace` at the household repository checkout, then follow this walk.

''See'': the repo carries the corpus and genesis; the new state directory holds no prior vessel home.

''Proves'': founding needs no old CRDT document, migration, clear, or rebirth.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/prepare>>

!! 2 · Prepare the machine

All code runs in Docker. The host keeps the shell shim, hooks, mounts, editor, and custody copies. Use a local filesystem for both mounts; keep `/state` outside the repository and separate from `/workspace`, which points at the household repository checkout. The vessel image carries code and genesis, never household state. The app receives no Docker socket.

Set `LARES_STATE` to the first operator's vessel-home directory and `LARES_WORKSPACE` to the household's git checkout of this repository, which holds tracked `bags/` and receives `wikis/`. Export both paths in this shell before running the host preparation commands; alternatively, create the literal paths named in `lares-surface.env`. On Windows, keep both mounts inside the WSL distro. Keep a terminal attached for founding and custody credentials; the CLI refuses a credential act without a TTY.

''Do'': build the product image and create the two host directories.

```sh
export LARES_STATE="$HOME/lares-state"
export LARES_WORKSPACE="$PWD"
mkdir -p "$LARES_STATE"
docker build -f Dockerfile --target serve -t lares-surface:local .
```

Run these from the root of the household checkout; the state path shown stands as an example, and any local directory outside the checkout serves. Under PowerShell, assign `$env:LARES_STATE` and `$env:LARES_WORKSPACE` before calling the `.ps1` shim.

Use `tools/lares-container.sh` on Unix-like hosts or `tools/lares-container.ps1` on Windows. The shims read the same environment or `lares-surface.env` and route CLI work through the image.

''See'': Docker reports a successful image build; the state path exists and the workspace checkout holds tracked `bags/`.

''Proves'': the host has the image and durable homes required by the compose surface.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-hands>>

!! 2a · The operator's hands before the two-hearth day

Each act below waits on a person and a machine; no session performs one. Check each off in order, and read its ''See'' before the next.

# ''The registrar key'': limit it to the one household domain, switch on its API access, and place the two credential files outside both mounts on the always-on node. ''See'': the one-shot issuer completes its first live issuance.
# ''The router'': hand out the node's resolver address to the LAN. ''See'': a LAN device resolves the household names through it.
# ''The node's ports'': 53 (UDP and TCP), 443 (TCP) and 5353 (UDP, mDNS) stand free on the node's LAN interface (`ss -lntup`). ''See'': nothing else listens there.
# ''A WSL2 node'': WSL runs with `networkingMode=mirrored` and `dnsTunneling=false` in `%USERPROFILE%\.wslconfig`, so the node sits on the real LAN; with `firewall=true`, Windows Firewall governs WSL traffic, so inbound allow rules for UDP and TCP 53, TCP 443 and UDP 5353 stand on the private LAN profile. ''See'': the second machine pings the node's LAN address and reaches its port 443.
# ''The devices'': run `tools/capability-probe/` on the tablet and on one phone before browser custody hardens. ''See'': each device's measured row.
# ''One real-LAN discovery run'': mDNS offers its unauthoritative candidates on the household LAN. ''See'': the second machine lists the node.
# ''The second machine'': docker stands, and it pulls the product image by the digest the release manifest names. ''See'': `docker image inspect` shows that digest.
# ''The ember's anchor'': choose a hardware freshness anchor for unattended raises, or accept that every raise asks a person's confirm; the day needs neither, since reach stays LAN only.
# ''The release index'': push and sign it after the last genesis re-bake and the last wire change.
# ''The fresh start'', on both machines: move the old homes aside by rename, never delete (#/fresh-state).
# ''The dress rehearsal'' with synthetic names, every cold-key card read back with `lares nexus seal cold rehearse`; then the ceremony (#/second-operator).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/decisions>>

!! 3 · Decide names and custody before typing

Choose the household domain and DNS provider; name the host that the household resolver will answer. Choose a private persona label and an outward Handle for each operator. A seat needs no public Handle; choose its private chair label and where its cold-key paper rests. Name the custody holders and separate storage places before the CLI prints recovery cards. Keep domain credentials outside the vessel image, compose inputs, and state mount.

Synthetic examples below use `operator-one`, `Operator One`, and `hearth.home.example.test`. Replace each with household choices. A private label and a public Handle carry separate meanings, even when their text matches.

''Do'': write down the chosen labels, Handle, chair, domain, backup path, and custody destinations.

''See'': each decision names a holder, a location, or a persona act.

''Proves'': the founding commands can use deliberate household values rather than inventing them.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/rehearse>>

!! 4 · Rehearse in an isolated Docker project

The harbor witness gives each leg fresh scratch state and workspace, chooses its own Compose project, and removes only its own containers and scratch directories. It builds the local image if that tag does not exist. Give the project a unique name if another rehearsal already uses the default. Every boot rests at the floor holding no vessel key, so the witness opens each scratch vessel through the test custody door (`tools/unlock-staged-container.sh`); that door refuses any state directory a harness did not stage, and a household vessel opens on a terminal alone.

''Do'': run the harbor container walk.

```sh
HARBOR_PROJECT=founding-rehearsal tools/harbor-walk-witness.sh
```

''See'': `PASS` lines for both legs: the hearth founded before `up` (`found --hearth`, then `up`, the founding face standing open), the floor that holds no key, the unlock that composes a Lararium wearing the face, workspace projection, a place lifted to a hearth after `up` (a face refuses on a place with no seed charter; `found --hearth` lifts it once the vessel stops), and a final `PASS harbor walk`.

''Proves'': the shim routes a TTY-safe founding, persona, and corpus walk through the image under review. This rehearsal creates no keeper state.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/found>>

!! 5 · Found the first node

One act founds the hearth, before `up`: `found --hearth`. Under ONE human route, typed on the attached terminal (a passphrase, a paper pin, or both), it mints the custody (the vessel key, the serving key and the custody root), the place, and persona zero with its private label and a claim on one chair. It then mints that chair's own seat key, prints the chair's NEXT key as a cold-key card, and asks the card back before the charter moves. Only then does its one seat sign the inception image through the custody door's `seal.incept`, and the act writes the PRIVATE seed charter (one seat, threshold one, the card's digest committed) and persona zero's face, which binds the charter's Nexus AID: the device edge, the dyad scope and the face's cabal sentinel all name that AID. No keyring rides the act. A run that stops anywhere runs again and completes; a card that never reads back writes no charter and no face (`CardReadBackRefused`). `--name` carries persona zero's private label; `--chair` labels the chair privately.

''The card.'' It prints 64 hex characters and a checksum in groups of four, with its purpose, the chair's label, its generation and a short charter fingerprint. Write it onto paper, then type it back; three readings that never match refuse. No copy rests on the node, and the act's answer carries the card's digest and confirm phrase alone. A live card prints on a terminal and never into `--json`, a redirect or a log; on a marked scratch root alone the read-back stands in, and the JSON answer carries the staged card's code for the harness.

''Do'': on the first operator's machine, replace the synthetic values and run each line in order.

```sh
tools/lares-container.sh found --hearth --name 'operator-one' --chair 'first-chair'
tools/lares-container.sh persona new 0 --name 'operator-one' --handle 'Operator One'
tools/lares-container.sh up
tools/lares-container.sh vessel seed --apply --yes
tools/lares-container.sh vessel read
tools/lares-container.sh persona list --json
tools/lares-container.sh nexus seal show
tools/lares-container.sh meme list --recipe default --json
```

The second line declares persona zero's public Handle; skip it for a face that keeps none. The label and the Handle land on the sealed rows alone, before `up` or on a standing vessel: a face's persona plane rests in the clear on disk, so no name rides onto it until its rest seals (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store#/owed">>).

The host shim starts the container at `up`. The vessel stands holding no vessel key, so `up` then runs `vault unlock` on the attached terminal: type the custody passphrase, and the vessel opens its key, opens the serving key through the vessel key's own entry, and composes with what the keys open. Every restart returns the vessel to the floor; the shim unlocks again after each restart it performs, and with no terminal attached it names the command (`tools/lares-container.sh vault unlock`). `tools/lares-container.sh vault lock` drops the key by hand. A face created after `up` triggers the shim's automatic restart when the CLI reports `restand`; the operator never types a restart flag.

''A place that stands already lifts the same way.'' Stop the vessel (`tools/lares-container.sh vessel stop`), run `found --hearth` over the standing place, then `up`. A face on a place with no seed charter refuses by name and names the act. Bare `found` still founds a place with no face (a herm's rite). `lares fleet join` carries a joiner through offer, request, answer and land.

''The seed leaves the checkout as it stood.'' A carrier whose bytes say what its records render stands unchanged, whatever framing a hand gave it, so a first `vessel seed` writes no tracked file and `git status` in the checkout reads clean. The unlock adds no untracked file either: the records the daemon seeds from source never project (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/disk-projection#/bag-roles">>), so `wikis/` receives a file only where a person writes one. A later change to a record writes that one carrier in its canonical render.

''See'': the act prints `the hearth stands: nexus-<sha>`, the seed charter at one seat and threshold one, PRIVATE, the seat's fingerprint, and persona zero's edge bound to this hearth. `vessel seed` names each holding under `/workspace/bags` and ends `6/6 holding(s) converged` (the count follows the holdings the checkout carries); the two infrastructure holdings ride the diff-gated ingest, and every other holding walks the island's named road: the act creates the bag, loads the carriers onto that wiki's working layer, then moves each landed record into canon, since a write lands on a working layer and canon takes it by promotion (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/disk-projection#/bag-roles">>); a second run submits nothing for a bag that already matches. `vessel read` reports the live node; `persona list` marks index zero active; `nexus seal show` reads SEED with one seated key; the daemon recipe list returns seeded wiki entries.

''Proves'': one act founds the hearth's custody, place, persona zero and seed charter, the vessel seeds its corpus from the tracked bags, and it serves as the first operator's live node.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/reserve>>

!! 6 · Establish custody

Keep a recovery route for the persona root and for the seed charter. The CLI prints one-time recovery material at the acts that mint it. Write each card onto its intended medium and move it to the holder and place chosen in step 3. Never treat a displayed secret as a backup until the human has copied it and checked that copy.

''Do'': inspect the vault and the seal, rehearse the card, then cut persona zero's backup bundle.

```sh
tools/lares-container.sh vault status
tools/lares-container.sh nexus seal show
tools/lares-container.sh nexus seal cold rehearse
tools/lares-container.sh vault backup --face 0 --out /state/face-0.bundle
```

The seed's seat holds its own key, sealed on the node, and its NEXT key rests on the card alone. Rehearse the card before the paper leaves the room: `nexus seal cold rehearse` asks for one or more cards on the terminal (a blank line skips a chair), reads each against the digests the head committed, names the chair each would roll, and moves nothing. A card that answers no digest refuses by its place in the typing and never by a byte of its code. The charter grows or rolls only by the roll its card arms (`nexus seal rotate`, which asks each card back the same way); the quorum rite (`nexus rite quorum`) refuses on a hearth, `hearth-bound`, since a re-seat would mint another AID and orphan every relation bound to the first (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/nexus-scale">>).

''The backup bundle carries one face.'' `vault backup --face <N> --out <file>` runs on the standing vessel, unlocked and wearing that face. It asks the vessel's own route again, then a NEW route for the bundle (a passphrase typed twice, or a paper sheet read back), and writes the file; it never writes over a standing one. The bundle carries the face's root with its own lines, its key history and its content keys, and no key of any seat; the serving tree stays on its hearth. Move the file off the machine. Its memory section holds keys alone: the sealed bytes they open must survive elsewhere, and on a production vessel that section stands empty until the wire cutover opens a session signer (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-cut">>).

''A restore brings the face back as a recovery act'' onto a fresh hearth: found it first (`found --hearth`, which stands its own persona zero), then, on the standing vessel, `vault restore <file> --as 1`. The restored face takes a new seat under its fixed prefix and binds that hearth's seed charter. It lands at the handle-index its bundle named only where no root stands there; persona zero holds index zero, so a persona-zero bundle takes `--as <N>` at a free index and lands beside persona zero, never as it (`V5` of the roadmap's loops). `vault restore <file> --memory` lands the bundle's content keys once the vessel wears that face (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-restore">>).

The credential prompts need the attached terminal. The vessel key seals the keyhive archive, each face's veil archive and each face's anchors where they rest, and the custody slot tree travels with them. For the whole vessel, stop it, then make one consistent copy of the full state mount, including its custody slots and relation carriers; that copy plus the custody passphrase (or paper pin) restores the vessel whole.

''See'': `vault status` reports held slots, names each sealed row, and reads the serving tree among the rows that rest bare by law; `nexus seal show` names the one seat and the head; the rehearsal names the chair the card would roll; the backup prints which bags and versions the bundle holds.

''Proves'': the first operator has recorded the node's custody state and holds recovery material, the card and the bundle, apart from the running machine.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/threshold-attest-recovery>>

!! Recovery cards and the guardian signing boundary

`persona new` splits no root by default. `persona new <index> --name '<label>' --split-root` opts into the 2-of-3 memory split. No card restores a charter. Guardians and paper restore a face's key lineage; only key material restores its private documents (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/persona-policy#/distinct-recovery">>).

`persona new` prints no recovery material by default; `--split-root` opts into self-custody reconstruction material and prints its own warning about shares. `lares persona guardian show <index>` reads a face's prefix and set. `lares persona guardian offer --prefix <prefix>` cuts one guardian key for that face; the guardian keeps its card and hands over its offer. `lares persona guardian enrol <index> --threshold <k> <offer>…` lets the face's current key sign its typed first enrolment. The vessel keeps that set in a sealed `guardian-set` row and refuses a key another face holds. A later guardian rotation needs the guardian quorum; the root-card reader and the bundle's carriage of an enrolled set still stand owed.

▢ ''OWED — the catastrophe rite.'' A lost card at a two-seat Nexus ends that Nexus (#/reserve holds the cold-key rite). `stable-founding-road.mem` and `kahu.mem#/seed-and-nexus` track charter replacement and Nexus founding.

''Do'': compare each physical or offline copy with the CLI's printed destination instructions, then confirm an independent second location.

''See'': every required holder can locate the material assigned to them without access to the running node.

''Proves'': recovery material has an independent custody path. The CLI cannot prove that a human placed a card safely.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/household-reach>>

!! 7 · Establish household reach

Household HTTPS uses a DNS-01 wildcard certificate for a Kahu-held domain and one split-horizon resolver. The resolver answers each household name from one records file the operator keeps, one `<LAN address> <name>` line per machine (`LARES_DNS_RECORDS`, mode 0600 owned by the vessel's uid; `docker-compose.surface.yml` names how to check an edit and restart the resolver). Write each address plainly, with no octet led by a zero. A public record under the household zone stays unreachable from inside the house. mDNS offers a hint only. No device installs a certificate authority. Keep the DNS credential and TLS private key outside the vessel state and image.

''When one phone cannot reach the household name.'' The household name answers only from the household's own resolver, the one the router hands every device. A phone or a browser set to send its DNS questions somewhere else (encrypted DNS) never asks that resolver, so the household name fails on that device alone while it works on the others. At home, set the device back to the network's own DNS:

* ''Android'': Settings → Network & internet → Private DNS (on some phones: Connections → More connection settings → Private DNS). Choose ''Automatic'' or ''Off''. A named provider (a hostname typed there) skips the household resolver. The setting holds for every network the phone joins, so a person who wants a named provider away from home turns it back on when they leave.
* ''iPhone and iPad'': Settings → Wi-Fi → the ⓘ beside the household network → turn off ''iCloud Private Relay'' for that network alone. A DNS or VPN app, or an installed DNS profile, sends questions past the household resolver as well: pause it at home, or remove the profile under Settings → General → VPN & Device Management.
* ''Chrome'': Settings → Privacy and security → (Security →) Use secure DNS (the path differs a little between a phone and a computer) → ''With your current service provider'', or off. A named provider skips the household resolver.
* ''Firefox'': Settings → Privacy & Security → DNS over HTTPS → ''Default Protection'' or ''Off''. ''Increased'' or ''Max'' protection with a named provider skips the household resolver.

''See'': on that device, at home, the household name opens the vessel's page over `https` with no certificate warning. Away from home the household name answers nothing, by design.

▢ ''OWED — household HTTPS integration.'' The optional Compose profile supplies a terminator and split-horizon resolver. The operator still needs DNS-01 issuance and renewal, LAN binding and DHCP wiring, and a verified trusted origin on a household device. Carriers: `harbor.mem`, `secure-context.mem`, and `stable-founding-road.mem`.

''See'': the carriers describe the required DNS-01 wildcard, resolver, and terminator boundary; the road still tracks the integration.

''Proves'': the operator can distinguish the required design from a finished household service.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/gather-the-fleet>>

!! 8 · Add the first operator's browser, then tablet or phone leaves

The first operator starts with a browser leaf; a tablet or phone forms a separate leaf. Each device mints its own key and receives one face's fleet-join answer for that key. The answer transfers no seed; it carries the internal `device-admit/v1` edge, that face's certificates and grants, and its sealed address secret.

▢ ''OWED — browser and phone leaf ceremony.'' Browser crossing and render rungs run, and a leaf-side edit survives a reload. Fleet join runs on node and browser. The complete household phone walk still needs secure-origin boot, on-device key minting and a verified live leaf. Do not use a desktop seed on a phone or call an HTTP LAN page a secure context. Measure actual device capabilities with `tools/capability-probe/`. Carriers: `stable-founding-road.mem` (browser rungs and HARBOR), `secure-context.mem`, and `persona-circle.mem`.

''See'': no supported command sequence can yet show a household phone leaf online.

''Proves'': the runbook marks the actual boundary and keeps the per-device-key law intact.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/second-operator>>

!! 9 · The second operator founds a seed, then both found the Nexus

The second operator founds a separate SEED on their own machine (steps 2, 5 and 6), with their own state, persona, custody, seat and device keys. `lares nexus seal cofound open --release <manifest> --out <offer>` mints the first founder's seat and cadence keys and prints a cold successor card for read-back. Carry the offer file to the second vault. There, `lares nexus seal cofound join <offer> --out <join>` asks for the first seat's fingerprint as heard, mints the second founder's seat and cadence keys, prints its card, and half-signs the joint inception. Carry the join file back. The first founder runs `lares nexus seal cofound seal <join> --out <inception>`, types the second fingerprint as heard, signs, and files the charter. The second founder runs `lares nexus seal cofound seal <inception>` and files the same charter under the same AID. One signed cadence inception rides the joint founding; each hearth signs it. Each seed keeps `founding: null` and its own AID.

Each founder then consents to their own carriage contract. `lares nexus contract <nym> --nexus <aid> --out <file>` opens a partial; the other vault runs `lares nexus contract --from <file> --out <grown-file>` to judge and cosign it. Carry the grown file back so the opener lands the act too. `lares nexus revoke <nym> --nexus <aid> --out <file>` takes the same two-vault path. Each receiving vault judges against its own charter head and board before signing and again before landing. The independent two-root import, boot and restart witness still stands owed, along with the kapae and roll-anchor doors and later multi-chair rolls.

!!! Carriage into one household's seed (built)

This path admits the second operator as a carrier of the FIRST operator's household seed; it founds no Nexus and moves no phase. Neither operator gains a seat in the other's charter. Transfer every public file between workspaces with integrity intact.

''Do'': the second operator follows steps 2 and 5–6 on their own machine. The first operator exports the charter.

```sh
tools/lares-container.sh nexus seal export --no-json > "$LARES_WORKSPACE/founding-roster.mem"
```

Transfer `founding-roster.mem` to the second operator's workspace without changing its bytes. The second operator imports the first operator's charter and accepts carriage for its Nexus AID.

```sh
tools/lares-container.sh nexus seal import --carry /workspace/founding-roster.mem
tools/lares-container.sh nexus accept-carriage --nexus <nexus-aid>
```

The second operator gives the printed Nym and contract signature to the first operator. The first operator contracts that Nym and exports the resulting admit bundle.

```sh
tools/lares-container.sh nexus contract <nym> --sig <contract-signature> --json > "$LARES_WORKSPACE/admit-bundle.json"
tools/lares-container.sh nexus members --list
```

Transfer `admit-bundle.json` to the second operator's workspace without changing its bytes. The second operator takes that bundle.

```sh
tools/lares-container.sh nexus admit-take /workspace/admit-bundle.json
```

''See'': the second operator receives a signed contract-in; the first operator's member list names the second operator after the contract act.

''Proves'': both households retain independent roots and the first household seed records the second operator's carriage membership; no Nexus founding occurs.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/hosted-family-member>>

!! 10 · Host a family member's phone leaf

A further family member may ride as a user leaf under an operator's Nexus. That path still needs the same secure browser origin and per-device key property. It does not turn the family member into a second operator or transfer an operator seed.

▢ ''OWED — hosted phone-browser admission.'' The CLI supplies host and walker invite verbs. The complete phone-browser path still needs trusted HTTPS, on-device key minting, admission, and a verified live leaf. Carriers: `membership-doctrine.mem` and `stable-founding-road.mem`.

''See'': membership doctrine describes the user relation; the road tracks browser and foreign-leaf work still ahead.

''Proves'': the runbook distinguishes hosted family membership from operator-to-operator carriage.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/aftercare>>

!! 11 · Aftercare and the next road

Keep `/state` and `/workspace` backups distinct. The state backup holds vessel homes and custody; the workspace holds the editable corpus. Keep the custody passphrase or paper pin away from both. Renew the DNS-01 certificate through the operator-owned renewal route when that path lands. Keep the host shim and its hooks on the host; keep vessel code in the image.

A live vessel may continue with its local corpus while household HTTPS, browser leaves, or user admission remain owed. The road carries these build steps and the two-household live check. The sensorium runbook carries a separate later walk.

''Do'': record backup owners and locations, then follow `stable-founding-road.mem` for the remaining HARBOR and two-household work.

''See'': the operator can name the next owner and carrier for every owed slice.

''Proves'': the first node remains legible and recoverable while the household path advances.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/key-replacement-cost>>

!! Key replacement cost

Relationships acquired after founding raise the cost of replacing keys. Preserve custody before inviting another operator.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-growth-rite>>

!! Charter succession crossing

The built `nexus seal grow` ceremony records a later charter crossing. It spans key rotation and operator signatures; this founding stops before that crossing.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/identity-classes>>

!! Identity classes

A seat in a Nexus records a Kahu relation, held by a seat key apart from the persona root. The first operator and second operator each hold their own node identity and persona roots. Each browser device holds a device key minted on that device. A hosted family member holds a user leaf under an operator's Nexus. A carriage contract records an operator relation; a device admission records a device relation. No one class grants another class's seed.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/edges>>

